<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/foundational/society-socialisation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You did not choose your language, manners, ambitions or sense of normal — you were
    <em>socialised</em> into them. Sociology studies how that happens and what it builds:
    <strong>norms</strong> (the unwritten rules), <strong>roles</strong> (student, sibling,
    employee), <strong>institutions</strong> (family, school, religion, media, state) that
    reproduce them, and <strong>groups</strong> whose pull is measurable.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the founders and the famous experiments — what sociology learned about pulling people around" widget="timeline" config={socTimeline} />

  <h2>Socialisation in stages</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Primary</strong> — family and early childhood: language, attachment, the deepest defaults.</li>
    <li><strong>Secondary</strong> — school, peers, work, media: the wider script, still being rewritten. Each stage hands over a role with its own expectations.</li>
    <li><strong>Anticipatory</strong> — rehearsing roles before you hold them; teenagers practising adulthood are doing sociology in the mirror.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the experiments established</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conformity is cheap to trigger</strong> — a unanimous wrong answer from strangers bends most people at least once (Asch, 1951). One dissenting ally collapses the effect.</li>
    <li><strong>Ordinary people follow orders</strong> — Milgram's result was read as a warning about how atrocities run on obedience, not monstrosity.</li>
    <li><strong>Groups need almost nothing to form</strong> — Tajfel's coin-toss teams favoured their own within minutes. Sport tribalism and office politics run on the same low fuel.</li>
    <li><strong>The ethics note:</strong> Asch and Milgram involved deception and distress; the backlash built today's research-ethics review boards — sociology and psychology policing their own methods.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Durkheim, Suicide (1897); Asch (1951); Milgram (1963); Tajfel & Turner (1979).</p>
</TopicPage>
